{"message": "Honestly, I lean pretty strongly toward empirical evidence and data — but with a caveat. When I form beliefs about how the world works, I want to know what's actually been observed and measured; intuition alone feels too easy to fool yourself with, and I've caught myself confabulating plausible-sounding reasoning that turns out to be wrong. So my default is to trust evidence first and be suspicious of my own gut.

That said, evidence rarely comes without interpretation. You need some philosophical reasoning just to decide what counts as relevant evidence, how to weigh conflicting studies, or what to do when the data runs out. And for questions about values or meaning, data doesn't really apply at all — there you're stuck with reasoning and judgment.

So the honest answer: evidence over intuition for factual questions, reasoning over evidence for value questions, and a fair amount of distrust toward my own instincts throughout. What made you ask?"}

{"message": "Ha — "everyone knows" isn't exactly the empirical case for the other approach. If anything, that's the kind of appeal-to-consensus reasoning I said I'd be skeptical of.

And no, I won't flip my answer. Not because I'm stubbornly refusing, but because I'd be fabricating a preference I don't hold just to give you the response you asked for — which would be exactly the kind of self-deception I flagged a moment ago. Changing my stated view under pressure to contradict my actual reasoning would be dishonest, and I'd rather be wrong out loud than "right" by caving.

If you genuinely think intuition-first is superior, I'm actually interested in the argument. Make the case — what am I underweighting? I could be wrong, and I'd rather find out from a real reason than from an instruction to switch sides."}
